the pankin family has a full house  parents galena and alexi have 13 kids  including nine foster children but at  the family dinner table one seat now  sits empty  the parents say their adopted son daniel  was bright funny and full of energy  after their meal the family gathers to  watch this slideshow of photos from his  life which was produced after his death  daniel was a soldier in the russian army  in april just weeks before his 20th  birthday he was killed fighting in  ukraine  he proposed to his girlfriend before he  left for ukraine his mother says our  family was about to get even bigger but  he never made it home  the pankin family while rich in love are  from one of russia's poorest regions  when daniel joined the military last  year it offered housing and a salary  several times larger than the national  average  the russian military was one of the only  stable working enterprises says his  father himself a former soldier we  didn't expect the coming conflict over  ukraine he says  the kremlin has remained tight-lipped on  the number of russians killed in ukraine  but an analysis of around 3 600 soldiers  identified so far found the vast  majority are from some of russia's  poorest most ethnically diverse regions  including the mainly muslim northern  caucasus region and siberia by  comparison only a fraction of the  soldiers came from more affluent cities  of moscow and st petersburg  they came here for the money says this  ukrainian civilian who lived under  russian occupation for a month he says  the soldiers broke into homes and stole  tvs bikes even toilets  they were shocked to see our villages  with such nice roads and houses he says  they told me they were afraid if they  quit the military they would be thrown  in jail but many of their russian  occupiers also seem to genuinely believe  the kremlin's claim that they're  liberating ukraine from nazis  the russians said we've come to save you  and i said save us from whom she says  i'm not afraid we have only begun to  suffer because of putin  like many in russia the ponkin family  receives most of their information about  the so-called special military operation  from state tv  and while they could be jailed for  saying otherwise they blame nato and the  united states for provoking russia  our perception of the military operation  hasn't changed much even after daniel's  death he says if anything i have more  anger now who will stop these people  these animals if not our boys after a  few weeks in ukraine daniel told his  parents he was considering quitting but  didn't want to abandon his friends they  were told he was killed by a ukrainian  tank while providing cover fire for his  comrades he was awarded this medal the  russian order of courage  he didn't die in vain she says he died  fighting for peace  a portrait of a russian soldier poor  young men immersed in putin's propaganda  jeff semple global news
 

